      "dom_id": "question-12785",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $4{k}^{2}-36=0.$",
      "worked_solution": "Before we start our normal process of factoring, notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $4.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n4{k}^{2}-36=4({k}^{2}-9)\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({k}^{2}-9) & =0 \\\\ 4({k}^{2}-{3}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4(k-3)(k+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\nk-3=0\\,\\text{or}\\,k+3=0,\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nk=3\\,\\text{or}\\,k=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $k=-3,3.$ We can write this even more concisely as $k=\\pm 3.$",
