      "dom_id": "question-23553",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Use the difference of squares method to solve the equation $4{x}^{2}=25.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we move all of the terms to the left-hand side and collect the like terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2} & =25 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-25 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the difference of squares formula, we factor the left-hand side of our quadratic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-25 & =0 \\\\ (2x)^{2}-(5)^{2} & =0 \\\\ (2x-5)(2x+5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the zero product rule, we have\n\n$$\n2x-5=0\\,\\text{or}\\,2x+5=0.\n$$\n\nThen, we solve each equation separately:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-5 & =0 & \\, & \\, & 2x+5 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =5 & \\, & \\, & 2x & =-5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-\\frac{5}{2},\\frac{5}{2}.$ We can write this even more concisely as $x=\\pm \\frac{5}{2}.$",
